According to the ISO C++20 standard (`[expr.prim.lambda.capture]`), what is the behavior of the following translation unit?

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int x = 10;

int main() {
    int y = 20;
    auto f = [=]() mutable {
        x += 5;
        y += 5;
        return x + y;
    };
    std::cout << f() << " " << x << " " << y << "\n";
}
```

A] Compilation fails because `x` has static storage duration and cannot be modified inside a `mutable` lambda with `[=]` default capture
B] Prints `40 10 20` because `[=]` captures both `x` and `y` by copy
C] Prints `40 15 25` because `mutable` turns copy captures into reference captures
D] Undefined behavior due to modifying a global variable inside a lambda without explicit capture
E] Prints `30 15 20` because `y += 5` is discarded before `return`
F] Prints `40 15 20` because `[=]` only captures local automatic variables (`y`) by copy, whereas `x` has static storage duration and is referenced directly rather than captured

Consider the following C11 / C17 translation unit compiled with `-O2` (strict aliasing enabled, `-fstrict-aliasing`):

```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint32_t foo(uint32_t *a, float *b) {
    *a = 1;
    *b = 0.0f;
    return *a;
}
```

If a caller invokes `foo((uint32_t *)&x, (float *)&x)` passing the address of the same 32-bit storage location for both `a` and `b`, what does the ISO C standard (effective type and aliasing rules, `6.5p7`) permit the compiler to do, and what is the defined portable way in C99/C11 to reinterpret the bit pattern of a `float` as a `uint32_t`?

A] The compiler may assume `a` and `b` do not alias (since `uint32_t` and `float` are incompatible types and neither is a character type), fold `return *a;` to `return 1;`, and the caller invokes undefined behavior; portable bit-reinterpretation in C uses `memcpy(&u, &f, sizeof(u))` (or type-punning through a `union` in C99/C11)
B] The C standard requires the compiler to reload `*a` from memory whenever any pointer of the same `sizeof` is written, returning `0`
C] Casting `(float *)&x` is a compile-time constraint violation in C11 unless `volatile` is used
D] The compiler must emit a runtime pointer comparison `if ((void*)a == (void*)b)` at `-O2`
E] Both `uint32_t` and `float` share the same effective type because both are 32-bit scalar types, so `0` is guaranteed to be returned
F] Using `reinterpret_cast<uint32_t>(*b)` is valid in C11 and avoids strict aliasing violations

Consider a lock-free stack implemented in C/C++ using an atomic Compare-And-Swap (`CAS`) instruction on the `head` pointer, where popped nodes are recycled and may be re-pushed by a free-list allocator:

```c
Node* pop( _Atomic(Node*)* head ) {
    Node* old_head = atomic_load(head);
    while (old_head != NULL) {
        Node* next = old_head->next;
        if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(head, &old_head, next))
            return old_head;
    }
    return NULL;
}
```

What concurrency bug can corrupt the stack if Thread 1 is preempted right after reading `next = old_head->next` while Thread 2 pops `A`, pops `B`, frees `B`, and pushes `A` back onto the stack (where `A->next` now points to a different address or `B` has been reallocated), and which technique eliminates this hazard without blocking?

A] Priority inversion; eliminated by setting `SCHED_FIFO` on all worker threads
B] The **ABA problem**: Thread 1's `CAS` succeeds because `head` equals `A` again, setting `head` to the stale pointer `B` even though `B` was already popped and recycled; eliminated by double-width CAS (`DWCAS` / 128-bit `cmpxchg16b`) pairing the pointer with a monotonically incrementing **version tag** (or by Hazard Pointers / epoch-based reclamation)
C] False sharing on the CPU L1 cache line; eliminated by aligning `Node` to 64 bytes (`alignas(64)`)
D] Livelock caused by `atomic_compare_exchange_weak` spurious failures; eliminated by replacing it with `atomic_compare_exchange_strong`
E] Torn reads of 64-bit pointers; eliminated by compiling with `-mno-red-zone`
F] Deadlock between the stack and heap lock; eliminated by calling `sched_yield()` inside the `while` loop

In the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) HotSpot implementation and Java Memory Model (`JLS §17.4`), consider the infamous broken double-checked locking pattern on a non-`volatile` field:

```java
class Singleton {
    private static Helper instance;
    public static Helper getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new Helper(42);
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Why can a thread calling `getInstance()` observe a non-`null` reference to `instance` whose non-`final` fields still have their default zeroed values (`0`) instead of `42`, and what happens if `Helper`'s fields are declared `final` (assuming `this` does not escape during construction)?

A] Without `volatile` on `instance`, there is no *happens-before* edge between the thread writing `instance = new Helper(42)` inside the synchronized block and another thread performing the unsynchronized outer read `if (instance == null)`, allowing the JIT compiler or CPU to reorder the store to `instance` before the stores inside `Helper`'s constructor; however, if `Helper`'s fields are declared `final` (and `this` does not escape), `JLS §17.5` freeze action at the end of the constructor guarantees that any thread observing the reference sees the initialized values of those `final` fields even via a data race
B] Because `synchronized` in Java only locks the thread stack and does not flush CPU caches unless `volatile` is also used
C] Even if all fields of `Helper` are `final`, `JLS §17.5` provides no visibility guarantee without `volatile` on `instance`
D] The bug only occurs on 32-bit JVMs due to non-atomic 64-bit object reference writes
E] Because the garbage collector relocates `Helper` before the constructor finishes executing
F] The outer `if (instance == null)` check throws a `ConcurrentModificationException` at runtime
